1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,747
[MUSIC]

2
00:00:15,982 --> 00:00:20,250
In our last lesson we talked
about the activity class.

3
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:25,670
And I showed you how one activity can
programmatically start another activity by

4
00:00:25,670 --> 00:00:29,690
first creating an intent object and
then by passing that

5
00:00:29,690 --> 00:00:35,310
intent to a method such as start
activity or start activity for result.

6
00:00:35,310 --> 00:00:38,755
Today, we are going to take
a deeper look at intents.

7
00:00:38,755 --> 00:00:41,240
We'll discuss how they are created and

8
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:45,750
processed as they're used in
starting other activities.

9
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:49,380
And I'll begin by presenting
the intent class itself.

10
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:51,610
I'll talk about how they're created,

11
00:00:51,610 --> 00:00:56,770
what fields they have, and
what information those fields contain.

12
00:00:56,770 --> 00:01:00,610
After that, I'll talk about the two
ways in which Android decides which

13
00:01:00,610 --> 00:01:05,850
activities should be started when a method
such as start activity is called.

14
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:09,020
First, I'll talk about
explicit activation in which

15
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:13,800
intents specifically name
the activity they want to start.

16
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:18,570
And second, I'll start about implicit
activation, where intents don't select

17
00:01:18,570 --> 00:01:24,150
a target activity but instead describe
the operation they want to be performed.

18
00:01:24,150 --> 00:01:26,620
After which Android finds and

19
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:30,300
then starts the activity that
can perform that operation.

20
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:34,500
The intent class is basically
a data structure that serves at

21
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:36,850
least two purposes in Android.

22
00:01:36,850 --> 00:01:40,960
One, it can specify an operation
that you want to perform.

23
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:45,460
And two, it can represent an event that
has occurred in the system that you

24
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:47,609
want to notify other components about.

25
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:52,330
Today however we're going to
focus on just the first of these.

26
00:01:52,330 --> 00:01:56,390
Using intents to specify operations
that you want performed.

27
00:01:56,390 --> 00:01:58,910
And we'll leave the second of these,
using intents for

28
00:01:58,910 --> 00:02:03,929
event notification, for a later lesson
when we talk about broadcast receivers.

29
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:08,970
Now you can think of intents as
providing a kind of language for

30
00:02:08,970 --> 00:02:12,980
specifying operations that
you want to have performed.

31
00:02:12,980 --> 00:02:16,330
In essence, intents give you
an easy way to say things like,

32
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:18,720
I want to select a contact.

33
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:20,980
I want to take a photo.

34
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:22,740
I want to dial a phone number.

35
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:24,862
Or I want to display a map.

36
00:02:24,862 --> 00:02:26,140
So in practice,

37
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:30,910
intents are usually constructed by one
activity that wants some work to be done.

38
00:02:30,910 --> 00:02:35,570
And then Android uses that intent
to start another activity that can

39
00:02:35,570 --> 00:02:37,490
perform the desired work.

40
00:02:38,730 --> 00:02:41,880
So let's talk now about the kinds
of information that can be

41
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:44,210
specified within an intent.

42
00:02:44,210 --> 00:02:50,040
For example, we'll talk about
intent fields including action,

43
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:56,400
data, category, MIME type,
target component, extras, and flags.

44
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,540
An intent's action field is
a string that represents or

45
00:03:01,540 --> 00:03:04,810
names the operation to be performed.

46
00:03:04,810 --> 00:03:06,465
Some built in examples include,

47
00:03:06,465 --> 00:03:12,315
action_dial, which means I want
someone to dial a phone number.

48
00:03:12,315 --> 00:03:17,420
Action_edit, I want someone to display
some data for the user to edit.

49
00:03:18,562 --> 00:03:22,590
Action_sync, I would like
to synchronize some data on

50
00:03:22,590 --> 00:03:24,848
my device with data on a server.

51
00:03:24,848 --> 00:03:27,661
An action_main,

52
00:03:27,661 --> 00:03:32,020
I want to start an activity as
the initial activity of an application.

53
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:37,020
You can set an intent's
action in several ways.

54
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:43,320
For example, you can pass an action string
as a parameter to the intent constructor.

55
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:48,160
Alternatively you can create an empty
intent and then call the set action method

56
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:51,110
on it, passing the action
string again as a parameter.

57
00:03:52,295 --> 00:03:55,840
Intents also has a data field
which represents data that is

58
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:57,800
associated with the intent.

59
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:04,220
Now that data is formatted as
a uniform resource identifier, or URI.

60
00:04:05,740 --> 00:04:12,310
And one example of intent data is a
geo-schemed URI which indicates map data.

61
00:04:12,310 --> 00:04:16,560
You might also remember that we
saw something exactly like this in

62
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:20,440
earlier lessons as part of those
map location applications.

63
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:25,190
Another example is a tell schemed URI,

64
00:04:25,190 --> 00:04:27,180
indicating a phone number
that you want dialed.

65
00:04:28,420 --> 00:04:33,480
And note that the strings that represent
the underlying data are first being passed

66
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:39,890
through the Uri.parse method, which takes
that string and then returns a URI object.

67
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:44,700
You can set the intents
data in a variety of ways.

68
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:48,690
For instance, you can pass it
directly to the intents constructor.

69
00:04:48,690 --> 00:04:54,040
Or you can create an empty intent and then
use the setData method to set the data for

70
00:04:54,040 --> 00:04:54,620
that intent.

71
00:04:54,620 --> 00:04:59,720
The intent category represents
additional information about

72
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:04,070
the kinds of components that can handle or
should handle this intent.

73
00:05:04,070 --> 00:05:08,170
Some examples include category_browsable.

74
00:05:08,170 --> 00:05:14,100
Which means that the activity can be
invoked by a browser via a URI link.

75
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:16,350
Another example is category_launcher.

76
00:05:16,350 --> 00:05:22,740
Which means that the target activity can
be the initial activity of a task and

77
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:25,480
is listed in the top level
application launcher.

78
00:05:26,830 --> 00:05:28,980
Intents also have a type feel.

79
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:32,400
Which specifies the MIME
type of the intents data.

80
00:05:33,830 --> 00:05:37,080
Some examples of MIME types include,
various image formats.

81
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:46,620
Things like image/png for PNG images,
and image/jpeg or JPG for JPEG images.

82
00:05:46,620 --> 00:05:52,220
There's, there are also various
text formats, such as text/html for

83
00:05:52,220 --> 00:05:56,740
HTML text, or text/plain for
arbitrary text data.

84
00:05:56,740 --> 00:05:58,490
And of course,

85
00:05:58,490 --> 00:06:02,480
if you don't specify a MIME type,
Android will try to infer one for you.

86
00:06:04,990 --> 00:06:09,570
You can set an intents MIME type by
using the setType method passing in

87
00:06:09,570 --> 00:06:13,110
a string that represents
the desired mind type.

88
00:06:13,110 --> 00:06:15,130
You can also set both the data and

89
00:06:15,130 --> 00:06:19,130
the type together by calling
the setDataAndType method.

90
00:06:21,170 --> 00:06:26,560
Intents also have a component field that
identifies the intent's target activity.

91
00:06:28,260 --> 00:06:31,940
And you can set this field if you
know that there is exactly one

92
00:06:31,940 --> 00:06:34,710
activity that should always
receive this intent.

93
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:40,300
You can set the intent's target component
using one of the intent's constructors,

94
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:44,340
by passing in a context object,
and a class object

95
00:06:44,340 --> 00:06:48,710
which represents the activity that
should perform the desired operation.

96
00:06:48,710 --> 00:06:51,150
And we'll see a code example
of this in a few minutes.

97
00:06:52,560 --> 00:06:57,180
You can also create an empty intent and
then use one of the setComponent(),

98
00:06:57,180 --> 00:07:02,360
setClass(), or setClassName()
methods to set that target activity.

99
00:07:03,790 --> 00:07:06,750
Intents also have an extras field.

100
00:07:06,750 --> 00:07:10,430
Extras store additional information
associated with the intent.

101
00:07:10,430 --> 00:07:16,450
Now extras are effectively
a map of key-value pairs.

102
00:07:16,450 --> 00:07:20,320
So the target activity has
to know both the name and

103
00:07:20,320 --> 00:07:23,279
the type of any extra data
that it intends to use.

104
00:07:25,020 --> 00:07:30,520
For example, the intent class
defines an extra called EXTRA_EMAIL.

105
00:07:30,520 --> 00:07:36,190
And this extra is used to pass a list
of recipients when sending email.

106
00:07:36,190 --> 00:07:37,490
Now as you can see here,

107
00:07:37,490 --> 00:07:42,950
this code creates an intent,
with the action Intent.ACTION_SEND.

108
00:07:42,950 --> 00:07:46,040
So we want to send some email.

109
00:07:46,040 --> 00:07:49,180
It also adds an extra to that intent.

110
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:52,950
The extra is named EXTRA_EMAIL.

111
00:07:52,950 --> 00:08:00,304
And in this case it's values include me,
aporter@cs.umd.edu,

112
00:08:00,304 --> 00:08:09,350
ceo@microsoft.com, potus@whitehouse.gov,
and mozart@musician.org.

113
00:08:09,350 --> 00:08:13,440
Now there are several different methods
for setting extras and the specific

114
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:17,800
form of these methods will depend on
the type of data you want to store.

115
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:19,460
So, for example, there's one method for

116
00:08:19,460 --> 00:08:23,790
storing a string, one method for
storing an array of floats, and so on.

117
00:08:26,080 --> 00:08:29,190
Another intent field is called flags.

118
00:08:29,190 --> 00:08:32,680
Flags represent information about
how the intent should be handled.

119
00:08:33,970 --> 00:08:40,470
Some built in examples include
flag_activity_no_history.

120
00:08:40,470 --> 00:08:45,090
Which means that when an activity
is started based on this intent,

121
00:08:45,090 --> 00:08:47,650
it shouldn't be put in the history stack.

122
00:08:47,650 --> 00:08:53,931
Another flag is FLAG_DEBUG_LOG_RESOLUTION
which tells Android to print out extra

123
00:08:53,931 --> 00:08:59,867
logging information when this intent is
being processed, and this is a great tool

124
00:08:59,867 --> 00:09:05,564
to use if your intents are not starting
up the activities that you want them to.

